===Etymology 1===
From {{inh|en|enm|policie}}, from {{der|en|fro|policie}}, {{m|fro|pollicie}} and {{m|fro|police}}, from {{der|en|la-lat|-}} {{m|la|politia||citizenship; government}}, classical {{der|en|la|polītīa}} (in Cicero), from {{der|en|grc|πολιτεία||citizenship; polis, (city) state; government}}, from {{m|grc|πολίτης||citizen}}. {{doublet|en|police|polis<id:police><t:police>|polity}}.

====Noun====
{{en-noun|~}}

# A [[principle]] of [[behaviour]], [[conduct]] which an [[entity]] ([[government]], organization, etc.) applies or seeks to follow, especially as formally expressed by an authoritative body. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#: {{ux|en|The Communist Party has a '''policy''' of returning power to the workers.}}
#: {{ux|en|It's company '''policy''' that all mobile phones are forbidden in meetings.}}
#: {{ux|en|Federal '''policy''' is constructed in complex ways, involving legislation, regulation, and lobbying.}}
# A document describing such a policy.
#: {{ux|en|Please print extra copies of this '''policy''' and post them where it will be easy for everyone to see.}}
# [[wise|Wise]], [[advantageous]], or politic [[conduct]]; [[prudence]], formerly also with connotations of [[craftiness]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Fuller Holy Warre|chapter=King Richard Taken Prisoner in Austria; Sold and Sent to the Emperour; Dearly Ransomed, Returneth Home|page=130|passage=[H]e &#91;{{w|Richard I of England}}&#93; was diſcovered in an inne in Auſtria, becauſe he diſguiſed his perſon not his expenſes; ſo that the very '''policie''' of an hoſteſſe, finding his purſe ſo farre above his clothes, did detect him: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Austen Pride and Prejudice|volume=II|chapter=XI|page=131|passage=These bitter accusations might have been suppressed, had I with greater '''policy''' concealed my struggles, and flattered you into the belief of my being impelled by unqualified, unalloyed inclination; {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|now|rare}} Specifically, political [[shrewdness]] or (formerly) [[cunning]]; [[statecraft]]. {{defdate|from 15th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Marlowe Jew of Malta|23|I|text=I, ''''policie'''? that's their profession,<br>And not simplicity, as they suggest.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1946|author=w:Bertrand Russell|title=History of Western Philosophy|section=I.25
|passage=Whether he believed himself a god, or only took on the attributes of divinity from motives of '''policy''', is a question for the psychologist, since the historical evidence is indecisive.}}
# {{lb|en|Scotland|now|chiefly|in the plural}} The [[grounds]] of a large country [[house]]. {{defdate|from 18th c.}}
#* '''1775''', {{w|Samuel Johnson}}, ''A Journey to the Western Islands of Scotland'' section on Aberbrothick
#*: Now and then about a gentleman’s house stands a small plantation, which in Scotch is called a ''policy'', but of these there are few, and those few all very young.
#* {{RQ:Stevenson Kidnapped|text=There was but one thing happened worth narrating; and that is the visit I had of Robin Oig, one of the sons of the notorious Rob Roy. He was sought upon all sides on a charge of carrying a young woman from Balfron and marrying her (as was alleged) by force; yet he stepped about Balquhidder like a gentleman in his own walled '''policy'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1955|author=w:Robin Jenkins|title=The Cone-Gatherers|publisher=Canongate|year_published=2012|page=36
|passage=Next morning was so splendid that as he walked through the '''policies''' towards the mansion house despair itself was lulled.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1980|title=AA Book of British Villages|publisher=Drive Publications Ltd|page=277, about [[Strachur]]|text=The house and its '''policies''' (grounds) lie between what were once two or three separate hamlets.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} The art of [[governance]]; [[political science]]. {{defdate|14th–18th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 5|act=I|scene=i|pages=69–70|pageref=70|passage=Liſt his diſcourse of Warre; and you ſhall heare / A fearefull Battaile rendred you in Muſique. / Turne him to any Cauſe of '''Pollicy''', / The Gordian Knot of it he will vnlooſe, / Familiar as his Garter: {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[state]]; a [[polity]]. {{defdate|14th–16th c.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A set political system; civil administration. {{defdate|15th–19th c.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A [[trick]]; a [[stratagem]]. {{defdate|15th–19th c.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Titus Andronicus Q1|act=II|scene=i|sig=&#91;C4&#93;|verso=1|page=21|passage=Tis '''pollicie''' and ſtratageme muſt doo / That you affect, and ſo muſt you reſolue, / That vvhat you cannot as you vvould [[achieve|atchiue]], / You muſt perforce accompliſh as you may: {{...}}}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Motive; object; inducement.
#* {{RQ:Sidney Arcadia|chapter=I|page=9|passage=I pray you (said he) honest men, what such right have you in me, as not to suffer me to doe with my self what I list? and what '''pollicie''' have you to bestow a benefite where it is counted an injury?}}

